WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.428
Firmware architecture is usually layered,
even when the codebase is small.

2
00:00:04.508 --> 00:00:07.516
A device may begin in immutable or
protected startup code,

3
00:00:07.596 --> 00:00:11.033
transition through one or more boot
stages, initialize hardware,

4
00:00:11.113 --> 00:00:13.630
load application firmware, configure
drivers,

5
00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.797
and then enter normal runtime behavior.

6
00:00:15.877 --> 00:00:19.191
Each stage establishes assumptions that
later code depends on.

7
00:00:19.271 --> 00:00:23.506
Boot ROM concepts, bootloaders,
application firmware, board support packages,

8
00:00:23.586 --> 00:00:25.980
device drivers, interrupt handlers,
timers,

9
00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:29.436
and communication stacks all have
different responsibilities.

10
00:00:29.516 --> 00:00:34.058
Security review improves when those
responsibilities are documented instead of being

11
00:00:34.138 --> 00:00:37.268
left as shared background knowledge among
a few developers.

12
00:00:37.348 --> 00:00:41.645
A trust boundary is a point where data,
control, authority, or assumptions change.

13
00:00:41.725 --> 00:00:44.917
Examples include data moving from a
peripheral into a driver,

14
00:00:44.997 --> 00:00:48.250
from an external communication stack into
application logic,

15
00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.214
from nonvolatile storage into runtime
configuration,

16
00:00:51.294 --> 00:00:53.504
from interrupt context into task context,

17
00:00:53.584 --> 00:00:57.206
or from privileged startup code into
ordinary application behavior.

18
00:00:57.286 --> 00:01:01.459
Memory-mapped registers deserve careful
treatment because they connect C code

19
00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:03.012
directly to hardware state.

20
00:01:03.092 --> 00:01:05.548
Reads may reflect changing device
conditions.

21
00:01:05.628 --> 00:01:07.592
Writes may change hardware behavior.

22
00:01:07.672 --> 00:01:10.557
Code that touches registers should be
small, reviewed,

23
00:01:10.637 --> 00:01:15.056
and organized so that higher-level logic
is not scattered across unrelated modules.

24
00:01:15.136 --> 00:01:17.530
Stored state is also part of the
architecture.

25
00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.783
Calibration data, feature flags,
identifiers, certificates, keys, counters,

26
00:01:21.863 --> 00:01:25.669
update metadata, and recovery markers can
change how the device behaves.

27
00:01:25.749 --> 00:01:29.493
Reviewers should ask where stored state
comes from, how it is validated,

28
00:01:29.573 --> 00:01:33.194
who may update it, and what happens when
it is missing or inconsistent.

29
00:01:33.274 --> 00:01:35.914
Boundary mapping makes firmware review
concrete.

30
00:01:35.994 --> 00:01:39.370
A simple diagram or table that lists
startup stages, interfaces,

31
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:41.843
storage regions, drivers, privileged
paths,

32
00:01:41.923 --> 00:01:44.870
and owners helps teams decide where
validation belongs,

33
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.650
where logging is useful, where secrets are
handled,

34
00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:49.940
and where failure behavior must be tested.
